# Generation Tracking & Version Lineage

Spec: GenerationTracking
Purpose:
  Define generation tracking and knowledge version lineage. Each
  knowledge entity (skill, concept, claim) carries a generation number
  that increments when the entity is revised, challenged successfully,
  or promoted. This enables knowledge ancestry tracing: "where did this
  knowledge come from?" and "what was replaced?"

Owns: lifecycle/generation.sdd

## Generation Concept

  A generation is a sequential integer marking a revision of a knowledge
  entity. Unlike semantic versioning (which tracks content changes),
  generation tracks the KNOWLEDGE LINEAGE — the chain of ancestors that
  led to the current form.

  @skill.retry
    generation: 4

  The entity has been revised 3 times from its original form (generations
  1→2→3→4). Each generation has a cause, a date, and a trigger.

## Generation Entity Format

  @generation.G-skill-retry

  entity:
    @skill.retry

  generations:
    - gen: 1
      at: 2026-08-15
      by: @discovery.D22
      reason: Initial discovery from payment-service

    - gen: 2
      at: 2026-08-22
      by: @task.T1023
      reason: Added provider-specific behavior from order-service

    - gen: 3
      at: 2026-08-28
      by: @challenge.C44
      reason: Challenge accepted — new provider guarantees idempotency

    - gen: 4
      at: 2026-09-01
      by: @task.T1045
      reason: Production tuning — backoff parameters adjusted

  lineage_graph:
    - @observation.O55
    - @discovery.D22
    - @candidate.retry-v1
    - @skill.retry-v1
    - @skill.retry-v2
    - @skill.retry-v3
    - @skill.retry-v4

## Knowledge Evolution Graph

  Observation
      ↓
  Discovery
      ↓
  Candidate
      ↓
  Skill
      ↓
  Project Rule
      ↓
  Challenge
      ↓
  Revised Skill
      ↓
  New Rule

  Example:
    @observation.O55
      discovered: 2026-08-15
      source: tmp/payment-service

    @discovery.D22
      observations: [@observation.O55, @observation.O56, @observation.O57]
      promoted_to: @candidate.retry-v1

    @candidate.retry-v1
      promoted_to: @skill.retry-v1

    @skill.retry-v1
      generation: 1

    @skill.retry-v2
      generation: 2
      supersedes: @skill.retry-v1

    @skill.retry-v3
      generation: 3
      supersedes: @skill.retry-v2
      superseded_by: @challenge.C44

    @skill.retry-v4
      generation: 4
      supersedes: @skill.retry-v3

## Generation Increment Triggers

  G1 — Initial creation: first time the entity is created
  G2 — Refinement: new evidence or use context expands the knowledge
  G3 — Challenge accepted: a @challenge.* was resolved in favor of change
  G4+ — Further evolution: any combination of the above

  [GEN-01] Every entity MUST start at generation 1.
  [GEN-02] Generation MUST increment by exactly 1 on each revision.
  [GEN-03] A generation increment MUST record the trigger entity (@discovery.*,
           @task.*, @challenge.*, @observation.*).
  [GEN-04] Generation history MUST be preserved — never overwrite.
  [GEN-05] Superseded generations MUST be marked DEPRECATED with supersedes pointer.
  [GEN-06] AI CANNOT create generation increments without evidence.

## Lineage Tracing

  Human: "Where did @skill.retry-v4 come from?"

  AI traces via @generation.* entity:
    v4 ← v3 ← v2 ← v1 ← candidate.retry-v1 ← discovery.D22 ← observation.O55,O56,O57

  This provides full provenance: the original observations, the clustering
  into a discovery, the candidate phase, and every subsequent revision.

  [LINEAGE-01] Every @skill.*, @concept.*, and @claim.* MUST have a
                @generation.* entity tracking its lineage.
  [LINEAGE-02] Lineage traces MUST include all ancestor entities.
  [LINEAGE-03] Broken lineage (missing ancestor) MUST generate a drift alert.

## Generation and Claim Lifecycle

  Claims also carry generations:

  @claim.C11
    generation: 1
    status: ACTIVE

  When challenged and accepted:
  @claim.C115
    generation: 2
    supersedes: @claim.C11
    status: PROPOSED → VALIDATED → ACTIVE

  The old claim @claim.C11 is marked SUPERSEDED.

